\caption(The actual decision at time four compares deadlines eight and ten. The earlier job runs; the old job keeps its remaining work and resumes.)
\alttext(A native diagram compares the unfinished T99 job due at ten with a new T7 job due at eight, and points from T7 to the actual execution unit four to five.)

> Define the model before applying the theorem

Each task i releases at times 0,T_i,2T_i,..., with positive integer
period T_i, nonnegative integer budget C_i and relative deadline T_i.
A job released at r is due at r+T_i. This model has one fully preemptive
processor, independent tasks, no blocking, no release jitter and zero
scheduling/context-switch overhead. The simulation executes the full
budget, rather than estimating worst-case execution times from data.
There is no natural observational dataset for these assumptions; explicit
task sets make the claims checkable.

EDF chooses the ready unfinished job with the smallest absolute deadline.
Ties use input task position, then release order within a task. External
task-id values do not affect priority. A zero-work job finishes at its
release. Execution occupies half-open integer units [t,t+1), and finishing
exactly at a deadline is on time. The finite simulation releases jobs
before H and checks deadlines through H, including H itself. A job due
after H can remain unfinished without a miss yet being observed.

Utilization is

\[
U=\sum_i C_i/T_i.
\]

Long-run demand makes U<=1 necessary: over a sufficiently long interval,
released work grows as U times its length, while capacity grows as the
length. For this implicit-deadline model it is also sufficient under EDF.
One proof sketch considers the first missed deadline d and the maximal
busy interval [a,d] serving jobs due no later than d. An implicit-deadline
task can contribute at most floor((d−a)/T_i) such jobs released within
that interval. Their total demand is at most (d−a)U, which cannot exceed
capacity when U<=1, contradicting a miss. The interval begins with no
carried job eligible for that deadline. These assumptions are essential;
U<=1 alone does not cover blocking, arbitrary deadlines or multiple CPUs.

EDF's priority rule is optimal for independent preemptible jobs: in a
feasible schedule, exchange its first differing execution unit with a
later unit assigned to the earliest-deadline ready job. That job moves
earlier; the displaced ready job moves no later than the earlier job's
deadline, which is no later than its own. Repeating the exchange yields
EDF without introducing a miss. The argument concerns ideal processor
service, rather than a real operating system's overheads.

The core's edf_schedulable() compares exact rational demand using integer
limbs, so equality and very small overloads do not depend on a floating
tolerance. rt_utilization() remains a floating display value. The exact
sum implementation uses unreduced denominators: worst-case O(N²) native
word operations and O(N) integer words for N int-sized task records.

> Preserve the original three-task hyperperiod

The original tasks are (period,cost)=(4,1),(6,2),(8,2). Their utilization
is 1/4+2/6+2/8=5/6, and their hyperperiod is lcm(4,6,8)=24. In one
hyperperiod they release 6+4+3=13 jobs and require 6+8+6=20 processor
units, leaving four idle units. The new core plotting function maps
opaque task labels to rows and renders the actual merged intervals.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/edf_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const std::vector<dm::algorithms::RTTask> tasks{{0,4,1},{1,6,2},{2,8,2}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::rt_trace(tasks,24);
  if(t.work.jobs_released!=13||t.work.jobs_completed!=13||t.work.busy_ticks!=20||t.work.idle_ticks!=4||t.schedule.deadline_missed)throw std::runtime_error("original hyperperiod identity failed");
  std::print("Original tasks: utilization {:.6f}; EDF schedulable {}; horizon 24; deadline missed {}.\n",dm::algorithms::rt_utilization(tasks),dm::algorithms::edf_schedulable(tasks),t.schedule.deadline_missed);
  std::print("{} jobs released; {} completed; {} busy units; {} idle units.\n\n",t.work.jobs_released,t.work.jobs_completed,t.work.busy_ticks,t.work.idle_ticks);
  std::print("| start | end | task |\n| ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& s:t.schedule.timeline){if(s.id==std::numeric_limits<std::size_t>::max())std::print("| {} | {} | idle |\n",s.start,s.end);else std::print("| {} | {} | T{} |\n",s.start,s.end,s.id);}
  std::print("\n| job | task | release | deadline | executed | completion | missed |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.jobs.size();++i){const auto& j=t.jobs[i];std::print("| {} | T{} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i,t.tasks.at(j.task_index).id,j.release,j.deadline,j.executed,j.completion,j.missed);}
  auto p=dm::algorithms::plot_rt_schedule(tasks,t.schedule);p.title("Original EDF hyperperiod: 20 busy units and 4 idle").size(1000,500).save_svg("build/edf_original.svg");
}
