\caption(All thirteen original jobs finish on time, including jobs due at boundary 24. Blank regions are idle intervals, not missing data.)
\alttext(A native three-row task timeline shows the original EDF schedule through time twenty-four, with blank gaps representing four idle units.)

At boundary 24 every released job has finished. The synchronous arrival
pattern then repeats, so this deterministic hyperperiod returns to the
same empty-ready state. That supplies a repeat argument for this task
set. A general clean finite prefix, without such a state argument, is
not proof that future deadlines will all be met.

> Dynamic priorities and the RMS bound

Rate-monotonic scheduling fixes priorities by period. EDF can instead
keep an older long-period job ahead of a newly released short-period job
when the older job's absolute deadline is nearer. For tasks (5,2),(7,4),
U=34/35<1, so EDF is feasible under the model. RMS misses a deadline:
its first long-period job needs four units but receives only three by
time seven. The figure compares both actual traces over hyperperiod 35.

The Liu-Layland sufficient RMS bound for N tasks is
N(2^(1/N)−1), tending to log(2), about 0.693. This is a guarantee
threshold over task sets, not a maximum utilization that RMS can
possibly schedule. Harmonic tasks (4,2),(8,4) demonstrate that distinction:
U=1 exceeds the two-task bound, yet both jobs finish over hyperperiod
eight and the state repeats. The numerical RMS bound screen is not an
exact necessary feasibility test and has floating rounding near its bound.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/edf_vs_rms.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const std::vector<dm::algorithms::RTTask> tasks{{0,5,2},{1,7,4}};
  const auto edf=dm::algorithms::rt_trace(tasks,35),rms=dm::algorithms::rt_trace(tasks,35,dm::algorithms::RTPolicy::rate_monotonic);
  if(!dm::algorithms::edf_schedulable(tasks)||edf.schedule.deadline_missed||!rms.schedule.deadline_missed)throw std::runtime_error("policy comparison failed");
  std::print("Comparison utilization {:.6f}; two-task RMS sufficient bound {:.6f}.\n\n",dm::algorithms::rt_utilization(tasks),dm::algorithms::rate_monotonic_bound(2));
  std::print("| policy | jobs | completed by H | busy units | missed deadlines through H | preemptions |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& [name,t]:std::vector<std::pair<std::string,dm::algorithms::RTTrace>>{{"EDF",edf},{"RMS",rms}})std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",name,t.work.jobs_released,t.work.jobs_completed,t.work.busy_ticks,t.work.deadline_misses,t.work.preemptions);
  const auto first=std::find_if(rms.jobs.begin(),rms.jobs.end(),[](const auto& j){return j.missed;});
  std::print("\nFirst RMS miss: T{}, release {}, deadline {}, eventual completion {}.\n",tasks.at(first->task_index).id,first->release,first->deadline,first->completion);
  const std::vector<dm::algorithms::RTTask> harmonic{{0,4,2},{1,8,4}};const auto h=dm::algorithms::rt_trace(harmonic,8,dm::algorithms::RTPolicy::rate_monotonic);
  std::print("Harmonic RMS: utilization {:.6f}; sufficient-bound screen {}; missed {}; {} of {} jobs complete by hyperperiod eight.\n",dm::algorithms::rt_utilization(harmonic),dm::algorithms::rate_monotonic_schedulable(harmonic),h.schedule.deadline_missed,h.work.jobs_completed,h.work.jobs_released);
  if(h.schedule.deadline_missed||h.work.jobs_completed!=h.work.jobs_released)throw std::runtime_error("harmonic repetition failed");
  auto a=dm::algorithms::plot_rt_schedule(tasks,edf.schedule);a.title("EDF: all deadlines met, U = 34/35");
  auto b=dm::algorithms::plot_rt_schedule(tasks,rms.schedule);b.title("RMS: long-period jobs can finish late");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2,1).add(a).add(b).size(1000,800).title("Actual dynamic and fixed priority schedules").fit_panels().save_svg("build/edf_vs_rms.svg");
}
