// result_end
\caption(All period and task-count sweeps satisfy their formulas. Captured entries and scheduling work have different meanings and exclude allocator bytes or real scheduler overhead.)
\alttext(Two native line plots show exact busy, idle and history growth across complete periods, and release-check versus history growth for zero-work tasks.)

With N tasks, H units, J releases and at most B ready unfinished jobs,
the engine requires O(H(N+1)+(H+J)log(B+1)) unit-word work, plus task
validation. It owns O(N+B) runtime counters/ready jobs and the merged
timeline, which can have H slices. Completed jobs leave the ready queue;
o unfinished job is discarded. Trace capture adds O(N+H+J) entries.
Ideal feasibility assumes no scheduling overhead; these implementation
counts do not estimate a real system's overhead or certify a measured WCET.

> Overdue work, the final boundary and capture limits

For tasks (2,3),(3,1), overload is unavoidable. At horizon two, the first
three-unit job is unfinished and due exactly then: this is already a
miss. Keeping the run going does not erase that job. It runs its third
unit in [2,3), completes late at three, and only then yields to the other
task's earlier-due waiting job. The old implementation overwrote
remaining work at a new release and omitted the check at H; the core
now retains backlog and includes that final boundary.

The owned trace counts N task records, H actual selected-job ids and J
released-job records. The complete N+H+J count and container geometry
are checked before owned copies/history allocation. The cap defaults
to one million; zero disables only the optional quota. It excludes
result-timeline copies, the working heap, scalar fields, vector capacity
and allocator bytes, so it is not a peak-memory bound. Failures return
no partial trace. Ordinary simulation/work calls have no optional quota.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/edf_boundaries.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::rt_trace({{0,2,3},{1,3,1}},6);
  std::print("Overload: {} jobs; {} completed; {} missed deadlines through H=6; selected jobs:",t.jobs.size(),t.work.jobs_completed,t.work.deadline_misses);for(auto id:t.tick_jobs)std::print(" {}",id);std::print(".\n\n");
  std::print("| job | task | release | deadline | executed | completion (-1 pending) | missed |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t id=0;id<t.jobs.size();++id){const auto& j=t.jobs[id];std::print("| {} | T{} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",id,t.tasks.at(j.task_index).id,j.release,j.deadline,j.executed,j.completion,j.missed);}
  const auto before=dm::algorithms::rt_trace({{7,2,3}},1),at=dm::algorithms::rt_trace({{7,2,3}},2,dm::algorithms::RTPolicy::earliest_deadline,4);
  int rejected=0;try{(void)dm::algorithms::rt_trace({{7,2,3}},2,dm::algorithms::RTPolicy::earliest_deadline,3);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  std::print("\nMiss before deadline H=1 {}; at deadline H=2 {}; exact entries {}; disabled-cap entries {}; one-short rejections {}/1.\n",before.schedule.deadline_missed,at.schedule.deadline_missed,at.captured_entries,dm::algorithms::rt_trace({{7,2,3}},2,dm::algorithms::RTPolicy::earliest_deadline,0).captured_entries,rejected);
  const int big=std::numeric_limits<int>::max();const std::vector<dm::algorithms::RTTask> tiny_overload{{0,big,big},{1,big,1}};
  std::print("Exact overload 1+1/INT_MAX: display {:.12f}; EDF schedulable {}.\n",dm::algorithms::rt_utilization(tiny_overload),dm::algorithms::edf_schedulable(tiny_overload));
  const auto maximum=std::numeric_limits<std::size_t>::max();int invalid=0;
  for(const auto& tasks:std::vector<std::vector<dm::algorithms::RTTask>>{{{0,0,1}},{{0,-1,0}},{{0,2,-1}},{{0,2,0},{0,3,0}},{{maximum,2,0}}})try{(void)dm::algorithms::edf_simulate(tasks,0);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++invalid;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::edf_simulate({},-1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++invalid;}
  std::print("Invalid period/work/duplicate-id/idle-id/horizon rejections {}/6; empty zero-horizon capture {}.\n",invalid,dm::algorithms::rt_trace({},0).captured_entries);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(64,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=2.5;box.height=1.8;box.font_size=22;
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<t.tick_jobs.size();++k){const auto id=t.tick_jobs[k];const auto& j=t.jobs.at(id);box.fill=j.task_index==0?dm::graphics::RGB{191,219,254}:dm::graphics::RGB{254,202,202};
    d.node(std::to_string(k),{3.2*k,0},std::format("[{},{}): job {}\nT{}\ndue {}",k,k+1,id,t.tasks.at(j.task_index).id,j.deadline),box);if(k)d.edge(std::to_string(k-1),std::to_string(k));}
  d.text({8,2.5},"Actual selected jobs: late work survives new releases",25).text({8,-2},"Job 0 finishes at 3, after its deadline at 2",23).save_svg("build/edf_boundaries.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Overload: 5 jobs; 2 completed; 5 missed deadlines through H=6; selected jobs: 0 0 0 1 2 2.

| job | task | release | deadline | executed | completion (-1 pending) | missed |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 0 | T0 | 0 | 2 | 3 | 3 | true |
| 1 | T1 | 0 | 3 | 1 | 4 | true |
| 2 | T0 | 2 | 4 | 2 | -1 | true |
| 3 | T1 | 3 | 6 | 0 | -1 | true |
| 4 | T0 | 4 | 6 | 0 | -1 | true |

Miss before deadline H=1 false; at deadline H=2 true; exact entries 4; disabled-cap entries 4; one-short rejections 1/1.
Exact overload 1+1/INT_MAX: display 1.000000000466; EDF schedulable false.
Invalid period/work/duplicate-id/idle-id/horizon rejections 6/6; empty zero-horizon capture 0.
\image(figures/edf_boundaries.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original overdue job retains its last unit at time two. All five released jobs miss their deadlines by boundary six, including jobs that subsequently finish.)
\alttext(A native six-unit chain shows selected jobs zero, zero, zero, one, two, two; the first job occupies the third unit even though its deadline was two.)

Unique task ids other than SIZE_MAX prevent confusing a task with idle
execution. Periods must be positive, budgets nonnegative and horizons
nonnegative; these checks apply even at horizon zero. Deadlines use a
wider integer than the input horizon to avoid release-plus-period overflow.
The native plotting function validates interval shape and ids but does
not certify demand or verdicts in arbitrarily edited caller results.

> What the examples establish

EDF's exact utilization test applies to the stated implicit-deadline
processor model. Actual job evidence explains preemption, confirms the
original hyperperiod and distinguishes dynamic deadlines from fixed
period priorities. Overload remains visible through late completions,
and boundary H belongs to deadline checking. The counter sweeps describe
this implementation's finite work and history, while timing guarantees
remain conditional on the ideal task model and its measured budgets.

> References

- C. L. Liu and J. W. Layland (1973), “Scheduling Algorithms for
  Multiprogramming in a Hard-Real-Time Environment”, Journal of the ACM
  20(1), 46–61, [doi:10.1145/321738.321743](https://doi.org/10.1145/321738.321743).
  The [NASA/JPL publication record](https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19730036159)
  describes the fixed-priority utilization bound and deadline-based
  dynamic priority result. The examples use the synchronous integer,
  implicit-deadline special case with free preemption.
